{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017uBqWxGkvBHbn5qrVfiyoe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing: When Does Error Injection Help LLM Agents? Boundary Conditions from a Failed Transfer to Embodied Planning\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t**Primary**: EMNLP 2026 Findings (Budapest, Oct 22–29) — 8-page long paper. Negative-result contributions accepted per CFP.\n5\t**Secondary**: ACL 2026 Findings if timeline permits.\n6\t\n7\t## Core Question\n8\tUnder what task-structural conditions does taxonomy-guided error injection improve vs. degrade LLM agent performance?\n9\t\n10\t## Thesis Statement\n11\tTaxonomy-guided error injection — a core component of PALADIN's +57pp Recovery Rate gain on ToolBench (though entangled with inference-time retrieval augmentation) — destabilizes embodied planning agent training without improving performance on ALFWorld. TaxEI and Random injection produce **identical** mean performance (58.3% vs Vanilla 67.3%, 5 seeds each), proving taxonomy labels provide zero benefit. Complete 5-seed dose-response reveals **no safe injection dose**: harm begins at 10% (-6.0pp), peaks at 25% (TaxEI nadir 52.7%), then **saturates** — 50% and Full produce similar harm (~58%) with no marginal worsening. The taxonomy penalty follows an **inverted-U curve**, peaking at 25% (-7.3pp TaxEI-Random gap) and vanishing at higher doses. At every concentration, training outcomes are a **lottery** (19-23pp within-dose range, bimodal seed distributions). Cross-model validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct reveals that **structured error injection (TaxEI) causes training instability across model architectures** — TaxEI recovery SD=8.6pp vs Random SD=2.1pp (3-seed), with 1/3 seeds triggering Qwen-like catastrophic degradation (59.6% vs baseline 73.1%). Random injection remains stable and even mildly beneficial (mean 76.6% > Vanilla 73.1%). This confirms the harm is not model-specific but a property of structured error patterns themselves: taxonomy-guided injection creates conflicting learning signals that destabilize training across architectures, while unstructured noise is benign or helpful.\n12\t\n13\t## Story Arc\n14\t\n15\t### 1. Motivation: Why error injection should work\n16\tError injection is a principled data augmentation strategy: expose the model to failure modes during training so it learns to avoid or recover from them. PALADIN (arXiv 2509.25238) demonstrated this for tool-use agents, using a ToolScan taxonomy of 7 failure types to generate 50K+ recovery-annotated trajectories plus inference-time retrieval of 55+ exemplars, boosting Recovery Rate from 32.76% to 89.68% on ToolBench (note: removing retrieval reduces the gain by ~30pp, so the SFT contribution alone is ~+27pp). A natural question: does the error-injection training component transfer to embodied planning, where agents execute multi-step plans in physical environments?\n17\t\n18\t### 2. Method: TaxEI — Taxonomy-guided Error Injection for Embodied Planning\n19\tWe designed a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld embodied planning:\n20\t- **P1** (step_order_swap): permute action ordering\n21\t- **P2** (sub_goal_deletion): remove sub-goals\n22\t- **C1** (context_truncation): truncate environmental context\n23\t- **C4** (observation_masking): mask observation feedback\n24\t\n25\tWe injected errors into 760 trajectories (from 501 GPT-5.2-generated successful trajectories), producing 4,671 injected variants. Three SFT conditions trained on Qwen3-8B: Vanilla (clean only, 4,358 samples), TaxEI (clean + taxonomy-tagged injections, 9,029 samples), Random (clean + unlabeled injections, 9,029 samples). TaxEI and Random differ only in whether the error tag contains the taxonomy label or a generic marker.\n26\t\n27\t### 3. Finding: Injection harms equally regardless of taxonomy; introduces catastrophic seed vulnerability\n28\t**Clean task performance (all 5-seed complete)**:\n29\t- Vanilla SFT: 67.3% ± 4.7pp (seeds 42/0/123/1/7) — stable, no catastrophic seeds\n30\t- TaxEI SFT: 58.3% ± 6.5pp (seeds 42/0/123/1/7) — **-9.0pp vs Vanilla, p=0.039 uncorrected, NS Bonferroni (p>0.017), Cohen's d=1.58**\n31\t- Random SFT: 58.3% ± 8.7pp (seeds 42/0/123/1/7) — **-9.0pp vs Vanilla, p=0.089 uncorrected, NS, Cohen's d=1.28**\n32\t\n33\t**CENTRAL FINDING**: TaxEI and Random produce IDENTICAL mean performance (58.3% = 137/235 tasks). Taxonomy labels provide zero benefit over random injection at the mean level. This directly refutes the hypothesis that structured error labeling improves training signal.\n34\t\n35\t**Catastrophic seed vulnerability**: Both injection methods produce exactly one ~47% outlier (TaxEI seed0=46.8%, Random seed1=46.8%). Vanilla never drops below 61.7%. Injection introduces a 1-in-5 risk of catastrophic training failure absent in clean training.\n36\t\n37\t**Variance gradient**: Vanilla SD (4.7pp) < TaxEI SD (6.5pp) < Random SD (8.7pp). Injection amplifies variance; taxonomy labels may mildly stabilize (TaxEI SD < Random SD), but both are worse than Vanilla. This reverses the 3-seed pattern (where TaxEI had highest SD). Formal variance tests needed (Levene's, n=5).\n38\t\n39\t**Dose-response ablation (fixed-size 4,358 total)** (D034+D035+D038: 全部 5 个剂量完整 5-seed):\n40\t| Dose | TaxEI Mean±SD (n) | Random Mean±SD (n) | Vanilla ref | TaxEI-Random Δ |\n41\t|------|-------------------|-------------------|-------------|----------------|\n42\t| 0% (vanilla) | — | — | 67.3±4.7 (5) | — |\n43\t| 10% | 61.3±8.2 (5) | 63.8±5.4 (5) | | -2.5pp |\n44\t| 25% | 52.7±7.6 (5) | 60.0±8.0 (5) | | **-7.3pp** |\n45\t| 50% | 57.4±9.5 (5) | 58.3±11.7 (5) | | -0.9pp |\n46\t| Full | 58.3±6.5 (5) | 58.3±8.7 (5) | | 0pp |\n47\t\n48\tPer-seed data:\n49\t- TaxEI 10%: 61.7/70.2/55.3/51.1/68.1. Random 10%: 68.1/66.0/55.3/68.1/61.7.\n50\t- TaxEI 25%: 48.9/46.8/46.8/63.8/57.4. Random 25%: 55.3/61.7/51.1/72.3/59.6.\n51\t- TaxEI 50%: 51.1/70.2/46.8/63.8/55.3. Random 50%: 57.4/46.8/70.2/70.2/46.8.\n52\t\n53\t**Unified dose-response narrative** (D035+D038, complete 5-seed all doses):\n54\t1. **Non-monotonic harm with 25% nadir + saturation**: Injection at ALL doses reduces mean and increases variance. Harm peaks at 25% (TaxEI nadir 52.7%), then partially rebounds: 50% (57.4%) ≈ Full (58.3%). Doubling injection from 50%→Full has zero marginal effect — ~50% is the saturation point beyond which additional errors no longer worsen outcomes.\n55\t2. **Inverted-U taxonomy penalty**: TaxEI trails Random at every dose, but the gap peaks at 25% (7.3pp), diminishes at 50% (0.9pp, d=-0.08 negligible), and vanishes at Full (0pp). At 10% the gap is 2.5pp. Taxonomy labels actively harm at intermediate doses where clean/injected balance may maximize conflicting learning signals.\n56\t3. **Universal training lottery**: All doses produce extreme seed variance (19-23pp within-dose range). Random 50% shows **bimodal** distribution: {46.8, 46.8, 57.4, 70.2, 70.2} — two distinct failure/success modes, not normal scatter. Report median+IQR alongside mean±SD for robustness.\n57\t4. **Practical implication**: No injection dose is \"safe.\" Even 10% produces measurable harm (-6.0pp TaxEI). Outcomes are unpredictable at every concentration level. The training lottery, not dosage, dominates performance variation.\n58\t\n59\t**Variance pattern** (exploratory, not claimable): SD peaks at 50% (TaxEI 9.5, Random 11.7) then **declines** at Full (6.5, 8.7). This is unexpected — Full dose actually reduces variance vs 50%. Possible explanation: at 50%, the model faces maximum ambiguity between clean and error patterns; at Full, the injection signal dominates and the model consistently adapts (poorly). Levene's test NS at n=5. Do not claim variance patterns without significance tests.\n60\t\n61\t**Training lottery at all doses**: Extreme outliers in every condition — TaxEI 10% s0=70.2% vs s1=51.1% (19pp range), Random 25% s1=72.3% vs s123=51.1% (21pp range), Random 50% bimodal (23pp range). Single-seed evaluations are fundamentally unreliable at any dose.\n62\t\n63\t**Taxonomy label penalty** (task-type-specific): TaxEI trails Random in aggregate. Per-task 3-seed analysis: taxonomy injection specifically harms **multi-step coordination tasks** — pick_cool_then_place (TaxEI 38.1% vs Random 57.1%, -19.0pp) and pick_two_obj_and_place (42.9% vs 57.1%, -14.3pp) — while leaving simple tasks largely unaffected (≤4.2pp gap for other 4 task types).\n64\t\n65\t**Recovery under injected errors (seed=42, 47 tasks, round-robin P1/P2/C1/C4)**:\n66\t- Vanilla: 61.7% | TaxEI: 59.6% | Random: 63.8%\n67\t\n68\t**Recovery ≈ Resilience (D041)**: Recovery eval rates match normal eval rates for all conditions (V: 61.7%=61.7%, T: 59.6%≈61.7%, R: 63.8%≈66.0%). Models succeed at the same base rate regardless of eval-time error injection — they passively ignore perturbations rather than actively recovering. This is **resilience** (noise tolerance from task competence), not **recovery** (error detection + correction). No training regime instills genuine error awareness.\n69\t\n70\t**Detection Rate**: 0% for all methods.\n71\t\n72\t**Taxonomy labels show no significant benefit**: TaxEI (56.7%) < Random (63.1%) < Vanilla (65.3%). TaxEI vs Random: paired t p≈0.034 (df=2, directionally consistent but low power).\n73\t\n74\t**Per-operator local/global split** (recovery eval, seed=42, verified from eval JSON, **exploratory — N=11-12 per operator**): C4_observation_masking (+9.1pp, 6→7/11), P1_step_order_swap (0.0pp, 7→7/12), C1_context_truncation (−8.3pp, 9→8/12), P2_sub_goal_deletion (−8.3pp, 7→6/12). NOT a smooth monotonic gradient — instead a binary split: the most local/observable operator (C4) shows TaxEI advantage; global/cascading operators (C1, P2) show equal disadvantage; P1 is neutral. **Critical caveat**: each delta is driven by ±1 task success/failure at N=11-12 — well within random noise. Fisher exact tests would be NS for all operators. This must be presented as exploratory/hypothesis-generating, not confirmatory. The directional pattern (local positive, global negative) is suggestive but the specific magnitudes (9.1pp, 8.3pp) carry false precision.\n75\t\n76\t**Variance tests**: Observed SD gradient: Vanilla 3.3pp < Random 7.0pp < TaxEI 8.6pp. F-test p=0.252 (NS, underpowered at df=2,2), but Levene's test p=0.085 (NS), Brown-Forsythe p=0.842 (NS), bootstrap P(SD_TaxEI > SD_Vanilla) = 0.688. All formal tests NS at n=3 — the variance gradient is a directional observation, not a statistically confirmed effect. Report as \"observed pattern, suggestive but underpowered.\"\n77\t\n78\t**Training-evaluation paradox** (≥25% injection only): At high injection ratios, training loss is lower (taxei_50%: 0.260, random_50%: 0.260 vs vanilla: 0.294) but eval performance is worse (51.1-57.4% vs 61.7%). However, at 10% injection, training loss is actually *higher* (0.308) than vanilla — the paradox only emerges above the dose-response threshold. This is consistent with the threshold effect: low injection doesn't change the training distribution enough to lower loss, while high injection causes the model to memorize error patterns. Literature support: Arpit et al. (2017) memorization dynamics, Zhou et al. (2023 LIMA) superficial alignment hypothesis.\n79\t\n80\t### 4. Analysis: Why it fails in embodied planning but works in tool-use\n81\t\n82\t**Important caveat**: The comparison with PALADIN involves multiple confounding variables beyond task structure (base model, training scale, training recipe, evaluation environment). We hypothesize task structure as the primary factor but cannot rule out other explanations without controlled cross-domain validation. The analysis below identifies the most plausible structural differences.\n83\t| Dimension | Tool-Use (PALADIN ✓) | Embodied Planning (TaxEI ✗) |\n84\t|-----------|----------------------|------------------------------|\n85\t| **Error observability** | Explicit — API returns error codes, timeouts, exceptions | Implicit — wrong action sequences look syntactically valid |\n86\t| **Recovery action space** | Bounded — retry, switch tool, modify arguments | Unbounded — must re-plan entire action sequence |\n87\t| **Error locality** | Single turn — one API call fails | Cascading — one wrong step invalidates all downstream |\n88\t| **Signal-to-noise in training** | 80% recovery / 20% clean (PALADIN) | 50% injected / 50% clean — injection overwhelms signal |\n89\t| **Training scale** | 50K+ trajectories | 9K trajectories (4.7K injected) |\n90\t| **Feedback granularity** | Per-tool success/fail signal | Sparse reward — only task completion |\n91\t| **Inference-time augmentation** | 55+ exemplar retrieval (removes retrieval → RR drops ~30pp) | None — pure SFT, no inference-time augmentation |\n92\t\n93\t**Core hypothesis**: In tool-use, errors are **discrete, observable, and locally recoverable** — making error-injected training a form of targeted curriculum. In embodied planning, errors are **diffuse, silent, and globally cascading** — making injected samples indistinguishable from noise that corrupts the correct action distribution.\n94\t\n95\t**Critical nuance on PALADIN**: PALADIN's +57pp gain includes inference-time retrieval of 55+ exemplars; removing retrieval reduces recovery by ~30pp. This means PALADIN's success is partially attributable to retrieval augmentation, not purely to error-injection training. Our comparison therefore tests error injection in its most isolated form (pure SFT, no retrieval), which may be a stricter — and more informative — test of whether the training signal itself transfers.\n96\t\n97\t### 4b. Cross-Model Validation: TaxEI instability is not model-specific (LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, 3-seed)\n98\t\n99\tTo test whether the TaxEI failure is Qwen-specific or a general property of structured error injection in embodied planning, we replicate the core experiment on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (3 seeds: 42/0/123). Training setup identical: Vanilla 4,358 samples (1,635 steps), TaxEI/Random 9,029 samples (3,387 steps).\n100\t\n101\t**LLaMA Recovery Rate (injected eval, 47 tasks)**:\n102\t| Condition | s0 (eval=42) | s42 (eval=42) | s123 (eval=123) | Mean±SD |\n103\t|-----------|:------------:|:-------------:|:---------------:|:-------:|\n104\t| Vanilla | 74.5% | 74.5% | 70.2% | 73.1±2.5% |\n105\t| TaxEI | 74.5% | 74.5% | **59.6%** | 69.5±8.6% |\n106\t| Random | 74.5% | 76.6% | **78.7%** | 76.6±2.1% |\n107\t\n108\t**LLaMA Clean Success Rate**:\n109\t| Condition | s0 | s42 | s123 | Mean±SD |\n110\t|-----------|:--:|:---:|:----:|:-------:|\n111\t| Vanilla | 72.3% | 72.3% | 72.3% | 72.3±0.0% |\n112\t| TaxEI | 76.6% | 76.6% | **61.7%** | 71.6±8.6% |\n113\t| Random | 74.5% | 76.6% | 76.6% | 75.9±1.2% |\n114\t\n115\t**Key findings**:\n116\t1. **TaxEI training instability is cross-model**: 1/3 seeds (s123) triggers catastrophic degradation on LLaMA (-13.5pp recovery vs vanilla-s123, -10.6pp clean). Pattern mirrors Qwen's consistent degradation but with higher variance (2/3 seeds appear neutral).\n117\t2. **Random injection is cross-model stable**: SD=2.1% (recovery), no catastrophic seeds, mean slightly above Vanilla (+3.5pp). This is the opposite of Qwen where Random also degraded.\n118\t3. **TaxEI vs Random divergence**: On LLaMA, TaxEI mean (69.5%) < Random mean (76.6%), gap = -7.1pp. The variance difference is stark: TaxEI SD=8.6% vs Random SD=2.1%. Structured error patterns create training instability that unstructured noise does not.\n119\t4. **Model-dependent severity, model-independent direction**: Qwen shows consistent TaxEI harm (-9pp, low variance); LLaMA shows intermittent TaxEI harm (mean -3.6pp, high variance with catastrophic 1/3 seed). The *direction* (TaxEI ≤ Random ≤ Vanilla for stability) holds across models; the *severity* varies.\n120\t\n121\t**Implication for paper story**: The finding upgrades from \"negative result on one model\" to \"structured error injection is fundamentally destabilizing for embodied planning across architectures.\" The taxonomy's structured perturbations create systematic biases that some training seed/model combinations amplify into catastrophic failure, while random noise averages out harmlessly.\n122\t\n123\t**Eval seed caveat**: s0/s42 use eval seed=42; s123 uses eval seed=123. Cross-seed aggregation (mean±SD) has a minor eval-seed confound (~2pp from eval randomness). Within each eval-seed group, horizontal (condition) comparisons are fully fair. The critical finding (taxei-s123 collapse) is a within-group horizontal comparison.\n124\t\n125\t### 5. Contribution: What this negative result gives the community\n126\t1. **Empirical boundary condition**: First cross-domain transfer test of taxonomy-guided error injection — and first reported failure. Contrasts with PALADIN's tool-use success and EEF's alternative approach of extracting positive signal from failures.\n127\t2. **Recovery-pairing disentanglement** (long paper NEW): By testing recovery-paired injection on ALFWorld, we isolate whether the negative result stems from missing recovery pairing or from fundamental task-structure differences. This is the first controlled test of recovery-paired vs unpaired error injection in embodied planning.\n128\t3. **Recovery vs resilience distinction** (long paper NEW): Recovery eval rates match normal eval rates — models passively ignore eval-time errors (resilience) rather than actively recovering (recovery). Combined with 0% detection rate, this shows error injection creates no form of error awareness in embodied planning.\n129\t4. **Training variance warning**: Error injection increases cross-seed variance 2.6× (SD 8.6pp vs 3.3pp, 5-seed confirmed). Single-seed evaluations of error injection methods are fundamentally unreliable.\n130\t7. **Cross-model instability** (long paper NEW): TaxEI structured injection destabilizes training on both Qwen3-8B (consistent -9pp) and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (intermittent, 1/3 catastrophic seed), while Random injection remains stable across both models. This separates the harm attribution: it is structured error patterns — not model weakness — that cause instability.\n131\t5. **No-safe-threshold dose-response + training lottery**: Complete 5-seed dose-response ablation (fixed data size, all 5 doses × 5 seeds) shows harm at every tested dose: 10% (-6.0pp TaxEI), 25% (nadir, -14.6pp TaxEI), 50% (-9.9pp TaxEI), Full (-9.0pp). Non-monotonic: harm peaks at 25%, partial rebound at higher doses. Taxonomy penalty follows inverted-U curve: 10% (-2.5pp) → 25% (-7.3pp peak) → 50% (-0.9pp) → Full (0pp). No injection dose is \"safe.\" Training lottery (19-23pp within-dose range) operates at all concentration levels.\n132\t6. **Failure mode taxonomy** (long paper NEW): Qualitative failure analysis categorizes how error injection changes model behavior — noise injection (incoherent plans) vs wrong-learning (systematic biases).\n133\t\n134\t## Core Claims (each backed by experimental evidence)\n135\t\n136\t- **Claim 1**: Error injection (both taxonomy-guided and random) degrades embodied planning performance with large effect size but marginal statistical significance.\n137\t  → Support: **5-seed data**: TaxEI 58.3%±6.5pp and Random 58.3%±8.7pp vs Vanilla 67.3%±4.7pp. Both injection methods show -9.0pp gap. Welch t: Vanilla vs TaxEI p≈0.039 (uncorrected), Vanilla vs Random p≈0.089. Bonferroni corrected (3 comparisons, α=0.017): all NS. Cohen's d: 1.58 (TaxEI) and 1.28 (Random) — large effects. Underpowered at n=5; the ~9pp degradation may be a real effect requiring larger N to confirm.\n138\t\n139\t- **Claim 2**: Taxonomy labels provide zero benefit over random injection — structured error labeling does not improve training signal.\n140\t  → Support: **TaxEI = Random = 58.3% = 137/235 tasks (5-seed, identical)**. Welch t: t=0.000, p=1.000. This is the paper's strongest finding: the careful design of a 4-operator error taxonomy with per-error-type labels achieves exactly nothing compared to unlabeled random injection. The taxonomy's structured perturbations are indistinguishable from noise at the mean level. Variance differs: TaxEI SD (6.5pp) < Random SD (8.7pp), suggesting taxonomy may mildly stabilize, but both are worse than Vanilla (4.7pp).\n141\t\n142\t- **Claim 3**: Error injection causes **generalization instability** with catastrophic seed vulnerability — training converges stably but evaluation produces unpredictable outcomes including catastrophic failures.\n143\t  → Support: **5-seed complete**: Training loss is extremely stable across seeds (SD=0.0007), yet eval SD: Vanilla 4.7pp < TaxEI 6.5pp < Random 8.7pp. Both injection methods produce exactly one catastrophic outlier (~47%): TaxEI seed0=46.8%, Random seed1=46.8%. Vanilla never drops below 61.7%. **Memorization-generalization dissociation** (D029/D033): Forward pass v2 (trajectory-level) confirms injected samples have lower loss than clean (all conditions), but eval is worse. Random memorizes 2.1× deeper than TaxEI (gap -0.078 vs -0.037) yet achieves identical eval — deeper memorization does not linearly predict eval harm. Dose-response loss gaps are non-monotonic (25% > 50%), not the monotonic pattern in v1 (which had a classification bug). D026 behavioral evidence (partial update: 3-seed V/T, 1-seed R, NOT balanced — full 5-seed trajectory collection pending): TaxEI produces ~2.2× more loop_repetition failures than Vanilla. Ratio stable across data subsets but absolute rates pending balanced data. **Observed pattern** (correlation, not established causation): memorization of error patterns + loop fixation at eval time + generalization collapse. Per-operator loss ranking is unstable across conditions, precluding strong per-operator causal claims. 1-in-5 catastrophic seed risk is absent from clean training.\n144\t\n145\t- **Claim 4**: Injection-trained models do not improve error recovery — observed \"recovery\" is passive resilience (ignoring errors), not active recovery.\n146\t  → Support: Recovery rate TaxEI 59.6% vs Vanilla 61.7% (seed=42, 47 tasks). Head-to-head: TaxEI gained 4, lost 5, net -1. Per-operator: TaxEI advantage only on C4_observation_masking (+9.1pp), disadvantage on C1 (-8.3pp) and P2 (-8.3pp). **D041 key insight**: Recovery eval rates match normal eval rates across all conditions (Vanilla 61.7%=61.7%, TaxEI 59.6%≈61.7%, Random 63.8%≈66.0%). Models succeed at the same rate whether or not errors are injected at eval time — they passively ignore perturbations rather than actively detecting and recovering from them. This reframes \"no recovery improvement\" as \"no recovery mechanism at all\": the models are **resilient** (robust to noise) rather than **recovery-capable** (able to detect and correct errors). The distinction matters because resilience is a byproduct of task completion ability, while recovery requires explicit error awareness that none of the training regimes instill.\n147\t\n148\t- **Claim 5**: Detection training via error injection is completely ineffective — models show zero error awareness.\n149\t  → Support: Detection rate = 0% across all three methods (Vanilla, TaxEI, Random). Combined with D041's recovery≈resilience finding: models neither detect errors explicitly (0% detection) nor respond to them implicitly (recovery rate = normal eval rate). Error injection during training fails to create any form of error awareness in the embodied planning domain.\n150\t\n151\t- **Claim 6**: The divergence from PALADIN's positive results reveals task-structural boundary conditions for error injection.\n152\t  → Support: Qualitative analysis contrasting error observability, recovery action space, error locality, feedback granularity, and inference-time augmentation between tool-use and embodied planning domains. Note: PALADIN's +57pp includes retrieval augmentation (~30pp contribution), making our pure-SFT comparison a stricter isolation of the training signal's value. Additionally, EEF (arXiv 2504.13145) succeeds on WebShop by **extracting good signal from failures** rather than injecting bad signal — suggesting the injection direction itself may be flawed for planning domains.\n153\t\n154\t- **Claim 7** (long paper NEW): Recovery-paired injection actively harms performance, definitively ruling out recovery pairing as PALADIN's missing ingredient.\n155\t  → Support: **TaxEI-RP 4-seed mean 47.9% ± 7.8pp** (s42=38.3%, s0=46.8%, s1=48.9%, s123=57.4%) vs TaxEI 58.3% vs Vanilla 67.3%. Recovery-paired training (14,437 samples = TaxEI 9,029 + recovery continuations 5,408) performs **10pp worse than unpaired TaxEI** and **19pp worse than Vanilla**. Adding recovery demonstrations does not teach active recovery — it adds noise that further corrupts the action distribution. Detection rate remains 0%. This definitively answers the PALADIN recovery-pairing confound: the failure is not due to missing recovery pairs, but to fundamental task-structural incompatibility. (D043)\n156\t\n157\t- **Claim 8** ~~(long paper NEW)~~: **CANCELLED (D040)**. WebShop replication infeasible — P1_StepOrderSwap incompatible with linear action space (search+click), taxonomy 4→3 makes cross-environment comparison inequivalent. Downgraded to Discussion section analysis of ALFWorld vs WebShop structural differences and why cross-environment replication is non-trivial.\n158\t\n159\t- **Claim 9** (long paper NEW): Structured error injection (TaxEI) causes **cross-model training instability**, while random injection remains stable — the harm is a property of structured error patterns, not model-specific.\n160\t  → Support: **LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct 3-seed replication**. TaxEI recovery mean 69.5%±8.6pp vs Random 76.6%±2.1pp vs Vanilla 73.1%±2.5pp. TaxEI variance 4× higher than Random (SD 8.6 vs 2.1). 1/3 seeds (s123) shows catastrophic TaxEI collapse: recovery 59.6% (vs vanilla 70.2%, Δ=-10.6pp; vs random 78.7%, Δ=-19.1pp). Other 2 seeds show TaxEI=Vanilla=74.5%. Random is consistently stable or mildly beneficial across all seeds. **Cross-model pattern**: Qwen shows consistent TaxEI harm (mean -9pp, moderate variance); LLaMA shows intermittent TaxEI harm (mean -3.6pp, extreme variance with 1/3 catastrophic). Direction (TaxEI ≤ Random for stability) holds across both models. This upgrades the finding from \"Qwen-specific negative result\" to \"structured injection is fundamentally destabilizing for embodied planning.\"\n161\t\n162\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n163\t\n164\t1. **\"Statistical power is limited.\"**\n165\t   → All three conditions now have **5 seeds**. Welch t: Vanilla vs TaxEI p=0.039 (uncorrected), Bonferroni NS (α=0.017). Cohen's d=1.58 (large). Power at n=5: 45.2%; need n=9 for 80%. We do not claim significance after correction but emphasize the large effect size (-9.0pp) and report all individual seed results. Bootstrap 95% CI: [+3.4, +15.8]pp. Bayes Factor 0.68 (inconclusive). The variance gradient (Vanilla 4.7 < TaxEI 6.5 < Random 8.7) is consistent across seeds.\n166\t\n167\t2. **\"ALFWorld has only 47 test tasks — too small for reliable conclusions.\"**\n168\t   → Valid concern. We explicitly compute statistical power and report confidence intervals. The 47-task limitation is an ALFWorld constraint shared by all prior work on this benchmark. With 5 seeds × 47 tasks = 235 episodes per condition, our pooled sample gives adequate power for the observed ~9pp effect (d≈1.4). WebShop replication was planned but cancelled (D040: P1 incompatible with linear action space); the structural incompatibility itself supports our task-structure boundary condition argument.\n169\t\n170\t3. **\"Maybe the taxonomy itself is poorly designed.\"**\n171\t   → Gate 1 (injection legality ≥ 80%) passed at 100% — all injected trajectories are structurally valid. Gate 2 (recovery quality ≥ 75%) passed at 97.5%. The taxonomy is functional; it just doesn't help training.\n172\t\n173\t4. **\"The training recipe might be suboptimal for injection data.\"**\n174\t   → The same recipe produces strong Vanilla results (67.3% ± 4.7pp vs 42.6% zero-shot baseline, +24.7pp, 5 seeds), confirming the recipe works. The degradation is specific to injection augmentation, not training methodology.\n175\t\n176\t5. **\"50% injection ratio is too high — you're drowning the signal.\"**\n177\t   → Comprehensively addressed with complete 5-seed fixed-size dose-response ablation at 10%/25%/50%/Full (all 4,358 total samples, all 5 doses × 5 seeds). No safe threshold exists: even 10% injection degrades performance (TaxEI -6.0pp). The harm profile is non-monotonic: monotonic degradation 0%→10%→25% (TaxEI nadir 52.7%), partial rebound 25%→50%→Full driven by training lottery. Taxonomy penalty follows inverted-U curve peaking at 25% (-7.3pp TaxEI-Random gap). The pattern holds for both TaxEI and Random, suggesting it is a general property of error injection.\n178\t\n179\t6. **\"Why is detection rate 0%? Evaluation might be flawed.\"**\n180\t   → Current eval checks for explicit error flagging in model output. Models may develop implicit error awareness (e.g., output hedging, re-planning) not captured by binary detection metrics. We discuss this limitation and propose alternative detection measures.\n181\t\n182\t7. **\"Why not compare directly on ToolBench?\"**\n183\t   → Our contribution is precisely the domain contrast. Running PALADIN on ALFWorld or TaxEI on ToolBench would strengthen the paper but requires substantial engineering. We list this as future work.\n184\t\n185\t8. **\"PALADIN uses error+recovery pairs; TaxEI uses standalone errors. Isn't recovery-pairing the real difference?\"** [Dream M+W]\n186\t   → **Directly tested and refuted** (D043). TaxEI-RP (recovery-paired, 14,437 samples) achieves 47.9% ± 7.8pp — **10pp worse** than unpaired TaxEI (58.3%) and **19pp worse** than Vanilla (67.3%). Recovery pairing not only fails to help, it actively harms. This is the paper's strongest evidence for the task-structure hypothesis: even mimicking PALADIN's recovery-pairing design cannot overcome the fundamental incompatibility of error injection with embodied planning.\n187\t\n188\t9. **\"Power analysis says n=9 needed but you only ran n=5.\"** [Dream W]\n189\t   → We acknowledge n=5 is underpowered (45.2%) for the observed effect at α=0.017 (Bonferroni). We report this transparently with power curves. The large effect size (d=1.58), consistent direction across all 5 seeds, and dose-response replication across 25 independent training runs provide converging evidence despite per-comparison underpowerment.\n190\t\n191\t10. **\"Data volume confound: Vanilla has half the training data.\"** [Dream W]\n192\t    → Vanilla uses 4,358 samples; TaxEI/Random use 9,029. We address this via dose-response ablation at fixed 4,358 total samples. At 10% injection, TaxEI still degrades (-6.0pp, 5-seed), confirming harm is from injection content, not volume. The full-scale comparison's volume asymmetry is acknowledged as a limitation.\n193\t\n194\t11. **\"Only tested on one model — maybe it's a Qwen-specific issue.\"**\n195\t    → Addressed with LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct 3-seed replication. TaxEI instability reproduces cross-model (LLaMA SD=8.6pp, 1/3 catastrophic seed). Random injection is stable on both models. The direction (TaxEI harmful/unstable, Random neutral/stable) is consistent across architectures; only severity differs (Qwen: consistent harm; LLaMA: intermittent but catastrophic when triggered). This rules out model-specific explanations and points to structured error patterns as the causal factor.\n196\t\n197\t## Key Differentiators vs PALADIN\n198\t\n199\tPALADIN (arXiv 2509.25238) succeeds because tool-use has four structural properties that embodied planning lacks:\n200\t\n201\t1. **Discrete error signals**: Tool calls return explicit success/failure codes. In ALFWorld, a wrong action (e.g., \"go to countertop 2\" when the object is on shelf 1) produces a valid-looking observation — the agent receives no signal that it erred.\n202\t\n203\t2. **Local recoverability**: A failed API call can be retried or substituted with an alternative tool. In planning, a wrong early step (e.g., heating before picking up) invalidates the entire downstream plan, requiring global re-planning rather than local retry.\n204\t\n205\t3. **Bounded action space for recovery**: ToolBench has a finite API catalog — recovery means selecting from known alternatives. ALFWorld's text-based action space is open-ended, making \"what to do after an error\" fundamentally harder to learn from examples.\n206\t\n207\t4. **Scale**: PALADIN trains on 50K+ trajectories with 80% recovery focus. TaxEI has 9K total samples with 50% injection. The lower data volume means less room for the model to separate error patterns from noise.\n208\t\n209\t**This contrast IS the paper's core contribution**: error injection is not universally beneficial for LLM agents — it requires discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors to serve as effective training signal rather than noise.\n210\t\n211\t## Note on v3→v4 Vanilla Discrepancy\n212\tv3 vanilla SFT achieved 74.5% (seed=42) while v4 three-seed mean is 65.3%. The 9.2pp gap is partially explained by: (1) v3 used different data volume (4,176 vs 4,358 samples due to format alignment changes); (2) seed variance (v4 seed=42 = 61.7%, seed=0 = 68.1%). Paper should briefly note this in supplement to preempt reviewer confusion about different vanilla numbers across experimental rounds.\n213\t\n214\t## Dream Review Findings (dream_20260515T020837)\n215\t\n216\t**Scores**: Best 2/5 (artifact: dream fabricated experiments), Median 3/5 (Borderline Accept), Worst 2.5/5 (Borderline Revise)\n217\t\n218\t**Confirmed action items (cross-tier consistent)**:\n219\t1. Related work must expand to 15-20 refs. Add: Reflexion (Shinn 2023), McCoy et al. (2020) seed-dependent generalization, Mosbach et al. (2021) fine-tuning stability, Madry et al. (2018) adversarial training, Bengio et al. (2009) curriculum learning, D'Amour et al. (2022) underspecification, ReAct (Yao 2023).\n220\t2. PALADIN recovery-pairing confound must be discussed prominently: PALADIN pairs errors WITH recovery demonstrations; TaxEI injects standalone errors without recovery. This is the most parsimonious alternative explanation.\n221\t3. Formal variance test: F-test p=0.252 (NS) is our strongest stat. Also compute Levene's test for robustness.\n222\t4. Paper structure for 4-page limit: Intro → Method → Main Results (Table 1 + dose-response) → Qualitative/Error Echo → Per-Operator Gradient → Discussion → Conclusion. Move training dynamics, recovery prompting to appendix.\n223\t\n224\t**Observation items (monitor in real reviews)**:\n225\t- Core novelty concern: \"seed variance unreliable\" is known — our per-operator gradient and error echo differentiate us\n226\t- Self-undermining: we use 3 seeds but findings suggest ≥5 needed — prominent limitation, consider power analysis\n227\t- Data volume confound: Vanilla 4,358 vs TaxEI/Random 9,029 — acknowledge in limitations (dose-response at 10% controls for this partially)\n228\t- EEF 2×2 framework: {positive, negative signal} × {tool-use, planning} could sharpen Discussion\n229\t\n230\t## Supplementary Experiments — Long Paper (8-page)\n231\t\n232\t### Done (from short paper phase)\n233\t- ~~**3-seed main conditions**~~: ✅ Vanilla 65.3%±3.3pp, TaxEI 56.7%±8.6pp, Random 63.1%±7.0pp.\n234\t- ~~**Dose-response 3-seed (25%/50%)**~~: ✅ Dual regime confirmed. 10% = seed=42 only.\n235\t- ~~**Recovery eval (seed=42)**~~: ✅ TaxEI 59.6%, Vanilla 61.7%, Random 63.8%. Detection 0%.\n236\t\n237\t### Priority 1 (required for 8-page long paper)\n238\t- ~~**5-seed extension** [Claims 1,3]~~: ✅ Done. Vanilla 67.3±4.7, TaxEI=Random=58.3%. D032.\n239\t- ~~**Training dynamics** [Claim 3]~~: ✅ Done. Forward pass v2 (trajectory-level). D027/D029/D033.\n240\t- ~~**Qualitative failure analysis** [Claims 1,6]~~: ✅ Done. TaxEI loop_repetition 2.2×. D026.\n241\t- ~~**Extended statistics** [Claims 1,3]~~: ✅ Done. Bootstrap CI, power, Bayesian. D030-D032.\n242\t- ~~**Dose-response 5-seed** [Claim 3]~~: ✅ Done. All 5 doses × 5 seeds complete. 10% (D034), 25% (D035/D036), 50% (D038). Inverted-U taxonomy penalty curve. ~24 GPU-h.\n243\t- **Recovery-paired injection** [Claim 7]: 🔄 Training in progress (4 seeds × 4 GPU). Datagen complete (711/760), 14,437 samples. ~16 GPU-h.\n244\t- ~~**WebShop replication** [Claim 8]~~: ❌ CANCELLED (D040). P1 incompatible with linear action space. Downgraded to Discussion.\n245\t- **Recovery eval multi-seed** [Claims 4,5]: 7/15 complete. Remaining 8/15 after TaxEI-RP. ~15 GPU-h.\n246\t\n247\t### Priority 1.5 (cross-model validation)\n248\t- ~~**LLaMA-3.1-8B cross-model replication** [Claim 9]~~: ✅ Done. 3 seeds × 3 conditions, all training + eval complete. TaxEI instability confirmed cross-model (SD=8.6%, 1/3 catastrophic). Random stable (SD=2.1%). ~36 GPU-h.\n249\t\n250\t### Priority 2 (strengthens further)\n251\t- **Scale ablation** [Claim 6]: 25K-50K trajectories. ~$30-50 API + ~20 GPU-h.\n252\t\n253\t### Estimated total: ~120 GPU-hours (~30h wall clock with 4 × 5090)\n254\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"271a98a0-08e2-4ead-80c1-74723b80f94f","uuid":"d5ad937f-f626-471f-b775-6e8137cf4029","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:05:16.994Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_2/projects/tax_error_inject_agent/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T153939/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing: When Does Error Injection Help LLM Agents? Boundary Conditions from a Failed Transfer to Embodied Planning\n\n## Target Venue\n**Primary**: EMNLP 2026 Findings (Budapest, Oct 22–29) — 8-page long paper. Negative-result contributions accepted per CFP.\n**Secondary**: ACL 2026 Findings if timeline permits.\n\n## Core Question\nUnder what task-structural conditions does taxonomy-guided error injection improve vs. degrade LLM agent performance?\n\n## Thesis Statement\nTaxonomy-guided error injection — a core component of PALADIN's +57pp Recovery Rate gain on ToolBench (though entangled with inference-time retrieval augmentation) — destabilizes embodied planning agent training without improving performance on ALFWorld. TaxEI and Random injection produce **identical** mean performance (58.3% vs Vanilla 67.3%, 5 seeds each), proving taxonomy labels provide zero benefit. Complete 5-seed dose-response reveals **no safe injection dose**: harm begins at 10% (-6.0pp), peaks at 25% (TaxEI nadir 52.7%), then **saturates** — 50% and Full produce similar harm (~58%) with no marginal worsening. The taxonomy penalty follows an **inverted-U curve**, peaking at 25% (-7.3pp TaxEI-Random gap) and vanishing at higher doses. At every concentration, training outcomes are a **lottery** (19-23pp within-dose range, bimodal seed distributions). Cross-model validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct reveals that **structured error injection (TaxEI) causes training instability across model architectures** — TaxEI recovery SD=8.6pp vs Random SD=2.1pp (3-seed), with 1/3 seeds triggering Qwen-like catastrophic degradation (59.6% vs baseline 73.1%). Random injection remains stable and even mildly beneficial (mean 76.6% > Vanilla 73.1%). This confirms the harm is not model-specific but a property of structured error patterns themselves: taxonomy-guided injection creates conflicting learning signals that destabilize training across architectures, while unstructured noise is benign or helpful.\n\n## Story Arc\n\n### 1. Motivation: Why error injection should work\nError injection is a principled data augmentation strategy: expose the model to failure modes during training so it learns to avoid or recover from them. PALADIN (arXiv 2509.25238) demonstrated this for tool-use agents, using a ToolScan taxonomy of 7 failure types to generate 50K+ recovery-annotated trajectories plus inference-time retrieval of 55+ exemplars, boosting Recovery Rate from 32.76% to 89.68% on ToolBench (note: removing retrieval reduces the gain by ~30pp, so the SFT contribution alone is ~+27pp). A natural question: does the error-injection training component transfer to embodied planning, where agents execute multi-step plans in physical environments?\n\n### 2. Method: TaxEI — Taxonomy-guided Error Injection for Embodied Planning\nWe designed a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld embodied planning:\n- **P1** (step_order_swap): permute action ordering\n- **P2** (sub_goal_deletion): remove sub-goals\n- **C1** (context_truncation): truncate environmental context\n- **C4** (observation_masking): mask observation feedback\n\nWe injected errors into 760 trajectories (from 501 GPT-5.2-generated successful trajectories), producing 4,671 injected variants. Three SFT conditions trained on Qwen3-8B: Vanilla (clean only, 4,358 samples), TaxEI (clean + taxonomy-tagged injections, 9,029 samples), Random (clean + unlabeled injections, 9,029 samples). TaxEI and Random differ only in whether the error tag contains the taxonomy label or a generic marker.\n\n### 3. Finding: Injection harms equally regardless of taxonomy; introduces catastrophic seed vulnerability\n**Clean task performance (all 5-seed complete)**:\n- Vanilla SFT: 67.3% ± 4.7pp (seeds 42/0/123/1/7) — stable, no catastrophic seeds\n- TaxEI SFT: 58.3% ± 6.5pp (seeds 42/0/123/1/7) — **-9.0pp vs Vanilla, p=0.039 uncorrected, NS Bonferroni (p>0.017), Cohen's d=1.58**\n- Random SFT: 58.3% ± 8.7pp (seeds 42/0/123/1/7) — **-9.0pp vs Vanilla, p=0.089 uncorrected, NS, Cohen's d=1.28**\n\n**CENTRAL FINDING**: TaxEI and Random produce IDENTICAL mean performance (58.3% = 137/235 tasks). Taxonomy labels provide zero benefit over random injection at the mean level. This directly refutes the hypothesis that structured error labeling improves training signal.\n\n**Catastrophic seed vulnerability**: Both injection methods produce exactly one ~47% outlier (TaxEI seed0=46.8%, Random seed1=46.8%). Vanilla never drops below 61.7%. Injection introduces a 1-in-5 risk of catastrophic training failure absent in clean training.\n\n**Variance gradient**: Vanilla SD (4.7pp) < TaxEI SD (6.5pp) < Random SD (8.7pp). Injection amplifies variance; taxonomy labels may mildly stabilize (TaxEI SD < Random SD), but both are worse than Vanilla. This reverses the 3-seed pattern (where TaxEI had highest SD). Formal variance tests needed (Levene's, n=5).\n\n**Dose-response ablation (fixed-size 4,358 total)** (D034+D035+D038: 全部 5 个剂量完整 5-seed):\n| Dose | TaxEI Mean±SD (n) | Random Mean±SD (n) | Vanilla ref | TaxEI-Random Δ |\n|------|-------------------|-------------------|-------------|----------------|\n| 0% (vanilla) | — | — | 67.3±4.7 (5) | — |\n| 10% | 61.3±8.2 (5) | 63.8±5.4 (5) | | -2.5pp |\n| 25% | 52.7±7.6 (5) | 60.0±8.0 (5) | | **-7.3pp** |\n| 50% | 57.4±9.5 (5) | 58.3±11.7 (5) | | -0.9pp |\n| Full | 58.3±6.5 (5) | 58.3±8.7 (5) | | 0pp |\n\nPer-seed data:\n- TaxEI 10%: 61.7/70.2/55.3/51.1/68.1. Random 10%: 68.1/66.0/55.3/68.1/61.7.\n- TaxEI 25%: 48.9/46.8/46.8/63.8/57.4. Random 25%: 55.3/61.7/51.1/72.3/59.6.\n- TaxEI 50%: 51.1/70.2/46.8/63.8/55.3. Random 50%: 57.4/46.8/70.2/70.2/46.8.\n\n**Unified dose-response narrative** (D035+D038, complete 5-seed all doses):\n1. **Non-monotonic harm with 25% nadir + saturation**: Injection at ALL doses reduces mean and increases variance. Harm peaks at 25% (TaxEI nadir 52.7%), then partially rebounds: 50% (57.4%) ≈ Full (58.3%). Doubling injection from 50%→Full has zero marginal effect — ~50% is the saturation point beyond which additional errors no longer worsen outcomes.\n2. **Inverted-U taxonomy penalty**: TaxEI trails Random at every dose, but the gap peaks at 25% (7.3pp), diminishes at 50% (0.9pp, d=-0.08 negligible), and vanishes at Full (0pp). At 10% the gap is 2.5pp. Taxonomy labels actively harm at intermediate doses where clean/injected balance may maximize conflicting learning signals.\n3. **Universal training lottery**: All doses produce extreme seed variance (19-23pp within-dose range). Random 50% shows **bimodal** distribution: {46.8, 46.8, 57.4, 70.2, 70.2} — two distinct failure/success modes, not normal scatter. Report median+IQR alongside mean±SD for robustness.\n4. **Practical implication**: No injection dose is \"safe.\" Even 10% produces measurable harm (-6.0pp TaxEI). Outcomes are unpredictable at every concentration level. The training lottery, not dosage, dominates performance variation.\n\n**Variance pattern** (exploratory, not claimable): SD peaks at 50% (TaxEI 9.5, Random 11.7) then **declines** at Full (6.5, 8.7). This is unexpected — Full dose actually reduces variance vs 50%. Possible explanation: at 50%, the model faces maximum ambiguity between clean and error patterns; at Full, the injection signal dominates and the model consistently adapts (poorly). Levene's test NS at n=5. Do not claim variance patterns without significance tests.\n\n**Training lottery at all doses**: Extreme outliers in every condition — TaxEI 10% s0=70.2% vs s1=51.1% (19pp range), Random 25% s1=72.3% vs s123=51.1% (21pp range), Random 50% bimodal (23pp range). Single-seed evaluations are fundamentally unreliable at any dose.\n\n**Taxonomy label penalty** (task-type-specific): TaxEI trails Random in aggregate. Per-task 3-seed analysis: taxonomy injection specifically harms **multi-step coordination tasks** — pick_cool_then_place (TaxEI 38.1% vs Random 57.1%, -19.0pp) and pick_two_obj_and_place (42.9% vs 57.1%, -14.3pp) — while leaving simple tasks largely unaffected (≤4.2pp gap for other 4 task types).\n\n**Recovery under injected errors (seed=42, 47 tasks, round-robin P1/P2/C1/C4)**:\n- Vanilla: 61.7% | TaxEI: 59.6% | Random: 63.8%\n\n**Recovery ≈ Resilience (D041)**: Recovery eval rates match normal eval rates for all conditions (V: 61.7%=61.7%, T: 59.6%≈61.7%, R: 63.8%≈66.0%). Models succeed at the same base rate regardless of eval-time error injection — they passively ignore perturbations rather than actively recovering. This is **resilience** (noise tolerance from task competence), not **recovery** (error detection + correction). No training regime instills genuine error awareness.\n\n**Detection Rate**: 0% for all methods.\n\n**Taxonomy labels show no significant benefit**: TaxEI (56.7%) < Random (63.1%) < Vanilla (65.3%). TaxEI vs Random: paired t p≈0.034 (df=2, directionally consistent but low power).\n\n**Per-operator local/global split** (recovery eval, seed=42, verified from eval JSON, **exploratory — N=11-12 per operator**): C4_observation_masking (+9.1pp, 6→7/11), P1_step_order_swap (0.0pp, 7→7/12), C1_context_truncation (−8.3pp, 9→8/12), P2_sub_goal_deletion (−8.3pp, 7→6/12). NOT a smooth monotonic gradient — instead a binary split: the most local/observable operator (C4) shows TaxEI advantage; global/cascading operators (C1, P2) show equal disadvantage; P1 is neutral. **Critical caveat**: each delta is driven by ±1 task success/failure at N=11-12 — well within random noise. Fisher exact tests would be NS for all operators. This must be presented as exploratory/hypothesis-generating, not confirmatory. The directional pattern (local positive, global negative) is suggestive but the specific magnitudes (9.1pp, 8.3pp) carry false precision.\n\n**Variance tests**: Observed SD gradient: Vanilla 3.3pp < Random 7.0pp < TaxEI 8.6pp. F-test p=0.252 (NS, underpowered at df=2,2), but Levene's test p=0.085 (NS), Brown-Forsythe p=0.842 (NS), bootstrap P(SD_TaxEI > SD_Vanilla) = 0.688. All formal tests NS at n=3 — the variance gradient is a directional observation, not a statistically confirmed effect. Report as \"observed pattern, suggestive but underpowered.\"\n\n**Training-evaluation paradox** (≥25% injection only): At high injection ratios, training loss is lower (taxei_50%: 0.260, random_50%: 0.260 vs vanilla: 0.294) but eval performance is worse (51.1-57.4% vs 61.7%). However, at 10% injection, training loss is actually *higher* (0.308) than vanilla — the paradox only emerges above the dose-response threshold. This is consistent with the threshold effect: low injection doesn't change the training distribution enough to lower loss, while high injection causes the model to memorize error patterns. Literature support: Arpit et al. (2017) memorization dynamics, Zhou et al. (2023 LIMA) superficial alignment hypothesis.\n\n### 4. Analysis: Why it fails in embodied planning but works in tool-use\n\n**Important caveat**: The comparison with PALADIN involves multiple confounding variables beyond task structure (base model, training scale, training recipe, evaluation environment). We hypothesize task structure as the primary factor but cannot rule out other explanations without controlled cross-domain validation. The analysis below identifies the most plausible structural differences.\n| Dimension | Tool-Use (PALADIN ✓) | Embodied Planning (TaxEI ✗) |\n|-----------|----------------------|------------------------------|\n| **Error observability** | Explicit — API returns error codes, timeouts, exceptions | Implicit — wrong action sequences look syntactically valid |\n| **Recovery action space** | Bounded — retry, switch tool, modify arguments | Unbounded — must re-plan entire action sequence |\n| **Error locality** | Single turn — one API call fails | Cascading — one wrong step invalidates all downstream |\n| **Signal-to-noise in training** | 80% recovery / 20% clean (PALADIN) | 50% injected / 50% clean — injection overwhelms signal |\n| **Training scale** | 50K+ trajectories | 9K trajectories (4.7K injected) |\n| **Feedback granularity** | Per-tool success/fail signal | Sparse reward — only task completion |\n| **Inference-time augmentation** | 55+ exemplar retrieval (removes retrieval → RR drops ~30pp) | None — pure SFT, no inference-time augmentation |\n\n**Core hypothesis**: In tool-use, errors are **discrete, observable, and locally recoverable** — making error-injected training a form of targeted curriculum. In embodied planning, errors are **diffuse, silent, and globally cascading** — making injected samples indistinguishable from noise that corrupts the correct action distribution.\n\n**Critical nuance on PALADIN**: PALADIN's +57pp gain includes inference-time retrieval of 55+ exemplars; removing retrieval reduces recovery by ~30pp. This means PALADIN's success is partially attributable to retrieval augmentation, not purely to error-injection training. Our comparison therefore tests error injection in its most isolated form (pure SFT, no retrieval), which may be a stricter — and more informative — test of whether the training signal itself transfers.\n\n### 4b. Cross-Model Validation: TaxEI instability is not model-specific (LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct, 3-seed)\n\nTo test whether the TaxEI failure is Qwen-specific or a general property of structured error injection in embodied planning, we replicate the core experiment on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (3 seeds: 42/0/123). Training setup identical: Vanilla 4,358 samples (1,635 steps), TaxEI/Random 9,029 samples (3,387 steps).\n\n**LLaMA Recovery Rate (injected eval, 47 tasks)**:\n| Condition | s0 (eval=42) | s42 (eval=42) | s123 (eval=123) | Mean±SD |\n|-----------|:------------:|:-------------:|:---------------:|:-------:|\n| Vanilla | 74.5% | 74.5% | 70.2% | 73.1±2.5% |\n| TaxEI | 74.5% | 74.5% | **59.6%** | 69.5±8.6% |\n| Random | 74.5% | 76.6% | **78.7%** | 76.6±2.1% |\n\n**LLaMA Clean Success Rate**:\n| Condition | s0 | s42 | s123 | Mean±SD |\n|-----------|:--:|:---:|:----:|:-------:|\n| Vanilla | 72.3% | 72.3% | 72.3% | 72.3±0.0% |\n| TaxEI | 76.6% | 76.6% | **61.7%** | 71.6±8.6% |\n| Random | 74.5% | 76.6% | 76.6% | 75.9±1.2% |\n\n**Key findings**:\n1. **TaxEI training instability is cross-model**: 1/3 seeds (s123) triggers catastrophic degradation on LLaMA (-13.5pp recovery vs vanilla-s123, -10.6pp clean). Pattern mirrors Qwen's consistent degradation but with higher variance (2/3 seeds appear neutral).\n2. **Random injection is cross-model stable**: SD=2.1% (recovery), no catastrophic seeds, mean slightly above Vanilla (+3.5pp). This is the opposite of Qwen where Random also degraded.\n3. **TaxEI vs Random divergence**: On LLaMA, TaxEI mean (69.5%) < Random mean (76.6%), gap = -7.1pp. The variance difference is stark: TaxEI SD=8.6% vs Random SD=2.1%. Structured error patterns create training instability that unstructured noise does not.\n4. **Model-dependent severity, model-independent direction**: Qwen shows consistent TaxEI harm (-9pp, low variance); LLaMA shows intermittent TaxEI harm (mean -3.6pp, high variance with catastrophic 1/3 seed). The *direction* (TaxEI ≤ Random ≤ Vanilla for stability) holds across models; the *severity* varies.\n\n**Implication for paper story**: The finding upgrades from \"negative result on one model\" to \"structured error injection is fundamentally destabilizing for embodied planning across architectures.\" The taxonomy's structured perturbations create systematic biases that some training seed/model combinations amplify into catastrophic failure, while random noise averages out harmlessly.\n\n**Eval seed caveat**: s0/s42 use eval seed=42; s123 uses eval seed=123. Cross-seed aggregation (mean±SD) has a minor eval-seed confound (~2pp from eval randomness). Within each eval-seed group, horizontal (condition) comparisons are fully fair. The critical finding (taxei-s123 collapse) is a within-group horizontal comparison.\n\n### 5. Contribution: What this negative result gives the community\n1. **Empirical boundary condition**: First cross-domain transfer test of taxonomy-guided error injection — and first reported failure. Contrasts with PALADIN's tool-use success and EEF's alternative approach of extracting positive signal from failures.\n2. **Recovery-pairing disentanglement** (long paper NEW): By testing recovery-paired injection on ALFWorld, we isolate whether the negative result stems from missing recovery pairing or from fundamental task-structure differences. This is the first controlled test of recovery-paired vs unpaired error injection in embodied planning.\n3. **Recovery vs resilience distinction** (long paper NEW): Recovery eval rates match normal eval rates — models passively ignore eval-time errors (resilience) rather than actively recovering (recovery). Combined with 0% detection rate, this shows error injection creates no form of error awareness in embodied planning.\n4. **Training variance warning**: Error injection increases cross-seed variance 2.6× (SD 8.6pp vs 3.3pp, 5-seed confirmed). Single-seed evaluations of error injection methods are fundamentally unreliable.\n7. **Cross-model instability** (long paper NEW): TaxEI structured injection destabilizes training on both Qwen3-8B (consistent -9pp) and LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct (intermittent, 1/3 catastrophic seed), while Random injection remains stable across both models. This separates the harm attribution: it is structured error patterns — not model weakness — that cause instability.\n5. **No-safe-threshold dose-response + training lottery**: Complete 5-seed dose-response ablation (fixed data size, all 5 doses × 5 seeds) shows harm at every tested dose: 10% (-6.0pp TaxEI), 25% (nadir, -14.6pp TaxEI), 50% (-9.9pp TaxEI), Full (-9.0pp). Non-monotonic: harm peaks at 25%, partial rebound at higher doses. Taxonomy penalty follows inverted-U curve: 10% (-2.5pp) → 25% (-7.3pp peak) → 50% (-0.9pp) → Full (0pp). No injection dose is \"safe.\" Training lottery (19-23pp within-dose range) operates at all concentration levels.\n6. **Failure mode taxonomy** (long paper NEW): Qualitative failure analysis categorizes how error injection changes model behavior — noise injection (incoherent plans) vs wrong-learning (systematic biases).\n\n## Core Claims (each backed by experimental evidence)\n\n- **Claim 1**: Error injection (both taxonomy-guided and random) degrades embodied planning performance with large effect size but marginal statistical significance.\n  → Support: **5-seed data**: TaxEI 58.3%±6.5pp and Random 58.3%±8.7pp vs Vanilla 67.3%±4.7pp. Both injection methods show -9.0pp gap. Welch t: Vanilla vs TaxEI p≈0.039 (uncorrected), Vanilla vs Random p≈0.089. Bonferroni corrected (3 comparisons, α=0.017): all NS. Cohen's d: 1.58 (TaxEI) and 1.28 (Random) — large effects. Underpowered at n=5; the ~9pp degradation may be a real effect requiring larger N to confirm.\n\n- **Claim 2**: Taxonomy labels provide zero benefit over random injection — structured error labeling does not improve training signal.\n  → Support: **TaxEI = Random = 58.3% = 137/235 tasks (5-seed, identical)**. Welch t: t=0.000, p=1.000. This is the paper's strongest finding: the careful design of a 4-operator error taxonomy with per-error-type labels achieves exactly nothing compared to unlabeled random injection. The taxonomy's structured perturbations are indistinguishable from noise at the mean level. Variance differs: TaxEI SD (6.5pp) < Random SD (8.7pp), suggesting taxonomy may mildly stabilize, but both are worse than Vanilla (4.7pp).\n\n- **Claim 3**: Error injection causes **generalization instability** with catastrophic seed vulnerability — training converges stably but evaluation produces unpredictable outcomes including catastrophic failures.\n  → Support: **5-seed complete**: Training loss is extremely stable across seeds (SD=0.0007), yet eval SD: Vanilla 4.7pp < TaxEI 6.5pp < Random 8.7pp. Both injection methods produce exactly one catastrophic outlier (~47%): TaxEI seed0=46.8%, Random seed1=46.8%. Vanilla never drops below 61.7%. **Memorization-generalization dissociation** (D029/D033): Forward pass v2 (trajectory-level) confirms injected samples have lower loss than clean (all conditions), but eval is worse. Random memorizes 2.1× deeper than TaxEI (gap -0.078 vs -0.037) yet achieves identical eval — deeper memorization does not linearly predict eval harm. Dose-response loss gaps are non-monotonic (25% > 50%), not the monotonic pattern in v1 (which had a classification bug). D026 behavioral evidence (partial update: 3-seed V/T, 1-seed R, NOT balanced — full 5-seed trajectory collection pending): TaxEI produces ~2.2× more loop_repetition failures than Vanilla. Ratio stable across data subsets but absolute rates pending balanced data. **Observed pattern** (correlation, not established causation): memorization of error patterns + loop fixation at eval time + generalization collapse. Per-operator loss ranking is unstable across conditions, precluding strong per-operator causal claims. 1-in-5 catastrophic seed risk is absent from clean training.\n\n- **Claim 4**: Injection-trained models do not improve error recovery — observed \"recovery\" is passive resilience (ignoring errors), not active recovery.\n  → Support: Recovery rate TaxEI 59.6% vs Vanilla 61.7% (seed=42, 47 tasks). Head-to-head: TaxEI gained 4, lost 5, net -1. Per-operator: TaxEI advantage only on C4_observation_masking (+9.1pp), disadvantage on C1 (-8.3pp) and P2 (-8.3pp). **D041 key insight**: Recovery eval rates match normal eval rates across all conditions (Vanilla 61.7%=61.7%, TaxEI 59.6%≈61.7%, Random 63.8%≈66.0%). Models succeed at the same rate whether or not errors are injected at eval time — they passively ignore perturbations rather than actively detecting and recovering from them. This reframes \"no recovery improvement\" as \"no recovery mechanism at all\": the models are **resilient** (robust to noise) rather than **recovery-capable** (able to detect and correct errors). The distinction matters because resilience is a byproduct of task completion ability, while recovery requires explicit error awareness that none of the training regimes instill.\n\n- **Claim 5**: Detection training via error injection is completely ineffective — models show zero error awareness.\n  → Support: Detection rate = 0% across all three methods (Vanilla, TaxEI, Random). Combined with D041's recovery≈resilience finding: models neither detect errors explicitly (0% detection) nor respond to them implicitly (recovery rate = normal eval rate). Error injection during training fails to create any form of error awareness in the embodied planning domain.\n\n- **Claim 6**: The divergence from PALADIN's positive results reveals task-structural boundary conditions for error injection.\n  → Support: Qualitative analysis contrasting error observability, recovery action space, error locality, feedback granularity, and inference-time augmentation between tool-use and embodied planning domains. Note: PALADIN's +57pp includes retrieval augmentation (~30pp contribution), making our pure-SFT comparison a stricter isolation of the training signal's value. Additionally, EEF (arXiv 2504.13145) succeeds on WebShop by **extracting good signal from failures** rather than injecting bad signal — suggesting the injection direction itself may be flawed for planning domains.\n\n- **Claim 7** (long paper NEW): Recovery-paired injection actively harms performance, definitively ruling out recovery pairing as PALADIN's missing ingredient.\n  → Support: **TaxEI-RP 4-seed mean 47.9% ± 7.8pp** (s42=38.3%, s0=46.8%, s1=48.9%, s123=57.4%) vs TaxEI 58.3% vs Vanilla 67.3%. Recovery-paired training (14,437 samples = TaxEI 9,029 + recovery continuations 5,408) performs **10pp worse than unpaired TaxEI** and **19pp worse than Vanilla**. Adding recovery demonstrations does not teach active recovery — it adds noise that further corrupts the action distribution. Detection rate remains 0%. This definitively answers the PALADIN recovery-pairing confound: the failure is not due to missing recovery pairs, but to fundamental task-structural incompatibility. (D043)\n\n- **Claim 8** ~~(long paper NEW)~~: **CANCELLED (D040)**. WebShop replication infeasible — P1_StepOrderSwap incompatible with linear action space (search+click), taxonomy 4→3 makes cross-environment comparison inequivalent. Downgraded to Discussion section analysis of ALFWorld vs WebShop structural differences and why cross-environment replication is non-trivial.\n\n- **Claim 9** (long paper NEW): Structured error injection (TaxEI) causes **cross-model training instability**, while random injection remains stable — the harm is a property of structured error patterns, not model-specific.\n  → Support: **LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct 3-seed replication**. TaxEI recovery mean 69.5%±8.6pp vs Random 76.6%±2.1pp vs Vanilla 73.1%±2.5pp. TaxEI variance 4× higher than Random (SD 8.6 vs 2.1). 1/3 seeds (s123) shows catastrophic TaxEI collapse: recovery 59.6% (vs vanilla 70.2%, Δ=-10.6pp; vs random 78.7%, Δ=-19.1pp). Other 2 seeds show TaxEI=Vanilla=74.5%. Random is consistently stable or mildly beneficial across all seeds. **Cross-model pattern**: Qwen shows consistent TaxEI harm (mean -9pp, moderate variance); LLaMA shows intermittent TaxEI harm (mean -3.6pp, extreme variance with 1/3 catastrophic). Direction (TaxEI ≤ Random for stability) holds across both models. This upgrades the finding from \"Qwen-specific negative result\" to \"structured injection is fundamentally destabilizing for embodied planning.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. **\"Statistical power is limited.\"**\n   → All three conditions now have **5 seeds**. Welch t: Vanilla vs TaxEI p=0.039 (uncorrected), Bonferroni NS (α=0.017). Cohen's d=1.58 (large). Power at n=5: 45.2%; need n=9 for 80%. We do not claim significance after correction but emphasize the large effect size (-9.0pp) and report all individual seed results. Bootstrap 95% CI: [+3.4, +15.8]pp. Bayes Factor 0.68 (inconclusive). The variance gradient (Vanilla 4.7 < TaxEI 6.5 < Random 8.7) is consistent across seeds.\n\n2. **\"ALFWorld has only 47 test tasks — too small for reliable conclusions.\"**\n   → Valid concern. We explicitly compute statistical power and report confidence intervals. The 47-task limitation is an ALFWorld constraint shared by all prior work on this benchmark. With 5 seeds × 47 tasks = 235 episodes per condition, our pooled sample gives adequate power for the observed ~9pp effect (d≈1.4). WebShop replication was planned but cancelled (D040: P1 incompatible with linear action space); the structural incompatibility itself supports our task-structure boundary condition argument.\n\n3. **\"Maybe the taxonomy itself is poorly designed.\"**\n   → Gate 1 (injection legality ≥ 80%) passed at 100% — all injected trajectories are structurally valid. Gate 2 (recovery quality ≥ 75%) passed at 97.5%. The taxonomy is functional; it just doesn't help training.\n\n4. **\"The training recipe might be suboptimal for injection data.\"**\n   → The same recipe produces strong Vanilla results (67.3% ± 4.7pp vs 42.6% zero-shot baseline, +24.7pp, 5 seeds), confirming the recipe works. The degradation is specific to injection augmentation, not training methodology.\n\n5. **\"50% injection ratio is too high — you're drowning the signal.\"**\n   → Comprehensively addressed with complete 5-seed fixed-size dose-response ablation at 10%/25%/50%/Full (all 4,358 total samples, all 5 doses × 5 seeds). No safe threshold exists: even 10% injection degrades performance (TaxEI -6.0pp). The harm profile is non-monotonic: monotonic degradation 0%→10%→25% (TaxEI nadir 52.7%), partial rebound 25%→50%→Full driven by training lottery. Taxonomy penalty follows inverted-U curve peaking at 25% (-7.3pp TaxEI-Random gap). The pattern holds for both TaxEI and Random, suggesting it is a general property of error injection.\n\n6. **\"Why is detection rate 0%? Evaluation might be flawed.\"**\n   → Current eval checks for explicit error flagging in model output. Models may develop implicit error awareness (e.g., output hedging, re-planning) not captured by binary detection metrics. We discuss this limitation and propose alternative detection measures.\n\n7. **\"Why not compare directly on ToolBench?\"**\n   → Our contribution is precisely the domain contrast. Running PALADIN on ALFWorld or TaxEI on ToolBench would strengthen the paper but requires substantial engineering. We list this as future work.\n\n8. **\"PALADIN uses error+recovery pairs; TaxEI uses standalone errors. Isn't recovery-pairing the real difference?\"** [Dream M+W]\n   → **Directly tested and refuted** (D043). TaxEI-RP (recovery-paired, 14,437 samples) achieves 47.9% ± 7.8pp — **10pp worse** than unpaired TaxEI (58.3%) and **19pp worse** than Vanilla (67.3%). Recovery pairing not only fails to help, it actively harms. This is the paper's strongest evidence for the task-structure hypothesis: even mimicking PALADIN's recovery-pairing design cannot overcome the fundamental incompatibility of error injection with embodied planning.\n\n9. **\"Power analysis says n=9 needed but you only ran n=5.\"** [Dream W]\n   → We acknowledge n=5 is underpowered (45.2%) for the observed effect at α=0.017 (Bonferroni). We report this transparently with power curves. The large effect size (d=1.58), consistent direction across all 5 seeds, and dose-response replication across 25 independent training runs provide converging evidence despite per-comparison underpowerment.\n\n10. **\"Data volume confound: Vanilla has half the training data.\"** [Dream W]\n    → Vanilla uses 4,358 samples; TaxEI/Random use 9,029. We address this via dose-response ablation at fixed 4,358 total samples. At 10% injection, TaxEI still degrades (-6.0pp, 5-seed), confirming harm is from injection content, not volume. The full-scale comparison's volume asymmetry is acknowledged as a limitation.\n\n11. **\"Only tested on one model — maybe it's a Qwen-specific issue.\"**\n    → Addressed with LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct 3-seed replication. TaxEI instability reproduces cross-model (LLaMA SD=8.6pp, 1/3 catastrophic seed). Random injection is stable on both models. The direction (TaxEI harmful/unstable, Random neutral/stable) is consistent across architectures; only severity differs (Qwen: consistent harm; LLaMA: intermittent but catastrophic when triggered). This rules out model-specific explanations and points to structured error patterns as the causal factor.\n\n## Key Differentiators vs PALADIN\n\nPALADIN (arXiv 2509.25238) succeeds because tool-use has four structural properties that embodied planning lacks:\n\n1. **Discrete error signals**: Tool calls return explicit success/failure codes. In ALFWorld, a wrong action (e.g., \"go to countertop 2\" when the object is on shelf 1) produces a valid-looking observation — the agent receives no signal that it erred.\n\n2. **Local recoverability**: A failed API call can be retried or substituted with an alternative tool. In planning, a wrong early step (e.g., heating before picking up) invalidates the entire downstream plan, requiring global re-planning rather than local retry.\n\n3. **Bounded action space for recovery**: ToolBench has a finite API catalog — recovery means selecting from known alternatives. ALFWorld's text-based action space is open-ended, making \"what to do after an error\" fundamentally harder to learn from examples.\n\n4. **Scale**: PALADIN trains on 50K+ trajectories with 80% recovery focus. TaxEI has 9K total samples with 50% injection. The lower data volume means less room for the model to separate error patterns from noise.\n\n**This contrast IS the paper's core contribution**: error injection is not universally beneficial for LLM agents — it requires discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors to serve as effective training signal rather than noise.\n\n## Note on v3→v4 Vanilla Discrepancy\nv3 vanilla SFT achieved 74.5% (seed=42) while v4 three-seed mean is 65.3%. The 9.2pp gap is partially explained by: (1) v3 used different data volume (4,176 vs 4,358 samples due to format alignment changes); (2) seed variance (v4 seed=42 = 61.7%, seed=0 = 68.1%). Paper should briefly note this in supplement to preempt reviewer confusion about different vanilla numbers across experimental rounds.\n\n## Dream Review Findings (dream_20260515T020837)\n\n**Scores**: Best 2/5 (artifact: dream fabricated experiments), Median 3/5 (Borderline Accept), Worst 2.5/5 (Borderline Revise)\n\n**Confirmed action items (cross-tier consistent)**:\n1. Related work must expand to 15-20 refs. Add: Reflexion (Shinn 2023), McCoy et al. (2020) seed-dependent generalization, Mosbach et al. (2021) fine-tuning stability, Madry et al. (2018) adversarial training, Bengio et al. (2009) curriculum learning, D'Amour et al. (2022) underspecification, ReAct (Yao 2023).\n2. PALADIN recovery-pairing confound must be discussed prominently: PALADIN pairs errors WITH recovery demonstrations; TaxEI injects standalone errors without recovery. This is the most parsimonious alternative explanation.\n3. Formal variance test: F-test p=0.252 (NS) is our strongest stat. Also compute Levene's test for robustness.\n4. Paper structure for 4-page limit: Intro → Method → Main Results (Table 1 + dose-response) → Qualitative/Error Echo → Per-Operator Gradient → Discussion → Conclusion. Move training dynamics, recovery prompting to appendix.\n\n**Observation items (monitor in real reviews)**:\n- Core novelty concern: \"seed variance unreliable\" is known — our per-operator gradient and error echo differentiate us\n- Self-undermining: we use 3 seeds but findings suggest ≥5 needed — prominent limitation, consider power analysis\n- Data volume confound: Vanilla 4,358 vs TaxEI/Random 9,029 — acknowledge in limitations (dose-response at 10% controls for this partially)\n- EEF 2×2 framework: {positive, negative signal} × {tool-use, planning} could sharpen Discussion\n\n## Supplementary Experiments — Long Paper (8-page)\n\n### Done (from short paper phase)\n- ~~**3-seed main conditions**~~: ✅ Vanilla 65.3%±3.3pp, TaxEI 56.7%±8.6pp, Random 63.1%±7.0pp.\n- ~~**Dose-response 3-seed (25%/50%)**~~: ✅ Dual regime confirmed. 10% = seed=42 only.\n- ~~**Recovery eval (seed=42)**~~: ✅ TaxEI 59.6%, Vanilla 61.7%, Random 63.8%. Detection 0%.\n\n### Priority 1 (required for 8-page long paper)\n- ~~**5-seed extension** [Claims 1,3]~~: ✅ Done. Vanilla 67.3±4.7, TaxEI=Random=58.3%. D032.\n- ~~**Training dynamics** [Claim 3]~~: ✅ Done. Forward pass v2 (trajectory-level). D027/D029/D033.\n- ~~**Qualitative failure analysis** [Claims 1,6]~~: ✅ Done. TaxEI loop_repetition 2.2×. D026.\n- ~~**Extended statistics** [Claims 1,3]~~: ✅ Done. Bootstrap CI, power, Bayesian. D030-D032.\n- ~~**Dose-response 5-seed** [Claim 3]~~: ✅ Done. All 5 doses × 5 seeds complete. 10% (D034), 25% (D035/D036), 50% (D038). Inverted-U taxonomy penalty curve. ~24 GPU-h.\n- **Recovery-paired injection** [Claim 7]: 🔄 Training in progress (4 seeds × 4 GPU). Datagen complete (711/760), 14,437 samples. ~16 GPU-h.\n- ~~**WebShop replication** [Claim 8]~~: ❌ CANCELLED (D040). P1 incompatible with linear action space. Downgraded to Discussion.\n- **Recovery eval multi-seed** [Claims 4,5]: 7/15 complete. Remaining 8/15 after TaxEI-RP. ~15 GPU-h.\n\n### Priority 1.5 (cross-model validation)\n- ~~**LLaMA-3.1-8B cross-model replication** [Claim 9]~~: ✅ Done. 3 seeds × 3 conditions, all training + eval complete. TaxEI instability confirmed cross-model (SD=8.6%, 1/3 catastrophic). Random stable (SD=2.1%). ~36 GPU-h.\n\n### Priority 2 (strengthens further)\n- **Scale ablation** [Claim 6]: 25K-50K trajectories. ~$30-50 API + ~20 GPU-h.\n\n### Estimated total: ~120 GPU-hours (~30h wall clock with 4 × 5090)\n","numLines":254,"startLine":1,"totalLines":254}}}
